<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeddah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jidda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeddah is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Made the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Uthman in the 7th century, it became the main gateway for pilgrims arriving by sea and a centre of Red Sea trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> took it in 1925, and it served as Saudi Arabia's diplomatic capital and remains its chief commercial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeddah.jpg" alt="Jeddah" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeddah_Waterfront,_Western_Saudi_Arabia_(2026).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The population reached 32 million in 2022, about 85% of them urban in Riyadh, <b>Jeddah</b> and Dammam, half under 25 and close to 42% immigrants; about 80% of private-sector employees are foreign, many under the kafala system that rights groups link to slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He hoped to conquer the Ethiopian highlands, and the province's seat moved between Massawa, Suakin and later <b>Jeddah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom controlled the Hejaz, its holy cities, the pilgrimage and the port of <b>Jeddah</b>, financed largely by British subsidies and pilgrim fees.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Kingdom of Hejaz</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sharifs kept order in Mecca and the pilgrimage roads, collected dues from pilgrims and <b>Jeddah's</b> port and balanced Bedouin tribes.</blockquote><a href="/state/sharifate-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a></li><li><blockquote>After Mecca and then <b>Jeddah</b> fell, Ibn Saud was proclaimed King of the Hejaz in January 1926, joining his two realms as the Kingdom of Hejaz and Nejd.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-nejd">Sultanate of Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
